 "lead": "Base language models acquire the referent of *you* in five ordered stages. Every base of 0.6B parameters or more trained on trillions of tokens reaches the first three. The fifth stage, tracking *you* after objects change hands, is **never reached zero-shot** by a public model up to 8B, at any token count measured up to 36T; its swap shift is negative in every one of them. Whether more tokens would get a small model there is unknown. From 14B to 1T parameters it is weak and nearly flat in 10 of the 11 bases with a public size: a zero-shot swap shift of +.01 to +.07, against +.27 to +.82 for static *you* in the seven of them where that is measured. In these models parameter count and depth grow together. **kotodama-tsumugi**, a 1.4B model run as 72 effective layers (looped depth, boundary-fitted attention residuals), separates them: it reaches stage 3 by **33B tokens** (annealed; on the constant-LR trunk by 40B), the token cost of OLMo-2 13B, against about 420B for OLMo-2 1B. Trained to 110B tokens, it still does not reach stage 5, but its zero-shot reading (−.021) is not inverted under the gauge rule, where every 1–2B public base whose static *you* binds, except Llama-3.2-1B, inverts.",
